However, the above assertion seems to be more complex than necessary based on the comment's intent. The comment suggests that after adding an element at a specific index, all elements from that index onwards should shift right, meaning the element at position `i+1` should be the element that was previously at position `i`. Here is a simplified version:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1) == \old(this.get(i));</code>

This assertion states that for every index `i` from the insertion point to the old size of the list, the element at `i+1` in the new list should be the element at `i` in the old list. Note that the loop condition is adjusted to `i < \old(this.size())` to ensure we don't go out of bounds when accessing `\old(this.get(i))`.

Given the nature of the operation, a more precise and simpler assertion would be:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1) == \old(this.get(i));</code>

But considering the exact wording of the comment, the most accurate assertion would be:

<code>assert \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size())-1; this.get(i+1) == \old(this.get(i));</code>

This ensures that the element at position `i+1` in the new list is the element that was at position `i` in the old list, up to the second-to-last element of the old list.
